; Managed by fedora-init (roles/ai/litellm). Installed with `semodule -i`
; (the play does it); `semodule -r litellm-http` removes it.
;
; What Fedora's policy denies the port-80 forwarder, rule for rule (each
; line answers one AVC seen live on 2026-09-20):
;
; 1. litellm-http.service runs under DynamicUser=, which implies
;    NoNewPrivileges= (systemd.exec(5): "cannot be disabled"). Under
;    no_new_privs the kernel lets an exec change SELinux domain only when
;    the policy grants `nnp_transition`, and Fedora grants init_t that
;    towards unconfined_service_t (which is how litellm and ollama run)
;    but NOT towards systemd_socket_proxyd_t — so systemd-socket-proxyd
;    stayed in init_t, a domain that may bind ports but may NOT connect to
;    an unreserved one such as 4000 ("Failed to connect to remote host:
;    Permission denied").
(allow init_t systemd_socket_proxyd_t (process2 (nnp_transition)))
; 2. systemd creates the listening socket with the service's computed
;    domain, and systemd_socket_proxyd_t may bind NO port by default
;    (verified via /sys/fs/selinux/access with the booleans off): the
;    socket unit fails with "Failed to create listening socket
;    (127.0.0.1:80): Permission denied". Port 80 is http_port_t.
(allow systemd_socket_proxyd_t http_port_t (tcp_socket (name_bind)))
; 3. Likewise it may connect to no port by default; 4000 is
;    unreserved_port_t (1024-32767).
(allow systemd_socket_proxyd_t unreserved_port_t (tcp_socket (name_connect)))
;
; Not the stock booleans systemd_socket_proxyd_bind_any /
; systemd_socket_proxyd_connect_any: they would cover rules 2 and 3 but
; not rule 1, and these three rules are narrower (two port types, not any).
